WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.980
Hey Wisecrack, Helen here.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.080
So, Jared's taking a breather this month,
which means I have the honor of telling you

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.920
guys all about one of the most beloved and
fan-requested animes in Wisecrack history:

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.020
Cowboy Bebop.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.020
Yes!

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.270
We're finally doing it!

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.490
So, what's taken us so long?

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:30.170
Well, when we sat down to analyze what makes
Cowboy Bebop so amazing, from it’s super-fluid

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:38.469
fight scenes to its insane grab-bag of a soundtrack,
we kept running into the same problem: every

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:43.270
episode is a kaleidoscope of references, philosophies,
narratives, and forms.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:46.510
It seemed downright impossible to pull on
just one thread.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:50.100
Then, around the beginning of season two,
we noticed this interstitial.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.729
Yeah, it might be hard to read, but it says:

00:00:53.729 --> 00:00:58.979
“Then… in 2071 in the universe, the bounty
hunters, who are gathering in spaceship Bebop,

00:00:58.979 --> 00:01:01.920
will play freely without fear of risky things.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.360
They must create new dreams and films by breaking
traditional styles.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.090
The work, which becomes a new genre itself,
will be called…

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.100
Cowboy Bebop.”

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.799
It’s those last two sentences about breaking
traditional styles and creating a new genre

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:17.409
that really gave us pause.

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:21.850
See, Cowboy Bebop isn’t just about great
characters and deep thoughts, it’s about

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.080
blurring the line between three distinct genres:
sci-fi, westerns, and noir.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.870
So, welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Cowboy
Bebop.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:45.829
And, you guessed it, spoilers ahead.

00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:51.899
First, a quick refresher course.

00:01:51.899 --> 00:01:57.630
Set in the year 2071, Cowboy Bebop follows
a group of starving bounty hunters who wander

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.479
from planet to planet looking for their next
meal ticket.

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:04.999
There’s Jet, a former detective who’s
rocking the Robocop look on his left arm;

00:02:04.999 --> 00:02:10.620
Spike, a modern take on the Marlboro Man mixed
with a tragic mob backstory; Faye, a femme

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:15.850
fatale dealing with gambling problems and
a serious case of amnesia; and, um, Ed.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:20.150
Who’s a brilliant hacker, but really, yeah,
she’s just Ed.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.410
Together, in more or less episodic fashion,
the team hunts down fugitives while shoveling

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.090
down some pretty meager meals:

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:31.570
"You don't call a meatless 'beef with bell
peppers... 'beef with bell peppers' do you?"

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.980
Aside from an ongoing fight with hunger, the
first thread you’ll notice in Cowboy Bebop

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.540
is its unique take on the sci-fi genre.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.581
The show challenges our idea of sci-fi by
presenting a future that really doesn’t

00:02:42.581 --> 00:02:43.930
have much to do with the future.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.330
Sure, there are all the familiar trappings
here.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.950
After the destruction of a giant space portal
that lets you travel the universe at near

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:56.160
light-speed, Earth has been left near uninhabitable,
and mankind has spread throughout the universe.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.910
And for those of you who grew up on things
like Star Wars or Gundam Wing, you’ll notice

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:01.680
a medley of conventions.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:02.770
Epic space battles?

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:03.770
Check.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.850
Terrifying, mysterious alien creatures?

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:06.850
Check.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:07.850
Space terrorists?

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:08.850
Sure, why not?

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:13.630
But the longer you inhabit the world of Cowboy
Bebop, the more you realize its brand of sci-fi

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.680
isn’t about portraying the future.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.790
In fact, it feels more like the 90’s than
it does 2071.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.940
Cities here aren’t the shining beacons on
the hill that futurism promised us; instead

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.630
they’re dirty, almost rundown.

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:29.920
Hell, there’s a whole space colony full
of drifters and riffraff.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.710
And technology isn’t exactly 70 years ahead
of its time, either.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:36.200
Take Session 7, for example – Heavy Metal
Queen.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.940
In it, we see the ins and outs of space transportation
through the lens of the best trucker there

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.630
is, VT.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:46.480
But it quickly becomes apparent that much
hasn’t changed for future trucking.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.680
Space trucks are still adorned with hood ornaments
their cabins are still cramped two-seaters

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.610
crammed with personal knicknacks.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:53.610
They even communicate via radio.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:54.610
"Thanks for the compliment.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:55.610
So, any info?"

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:00.460
"I passed by someone like the one you're looking
for about 10 minutes ago."

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:01.460
Radio, people.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:06.180
And when it’s time to wind down, they relax
at crappy rest stops which look identical

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.030
to thousands I’ve seen in West Tennessee.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.540
So, what’s going on here?

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.500
What are we to make of sci-fi that doesn’t
really use of the science part in a flashier

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:15.500
way?

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.880
As Wisecrack favorite, Ray Bradbury, tells
us, "Science fiction… is the fiction of

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:21.190
revolutions.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:25.870
Revolutions in time, space, medicine, travel,
and thought...

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:31.100
Above all, science fiction is the fiction
of warm-blooded human men and women sometimes

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.260
elevated and sometimes crushed by their machines."

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:39.370
In other words, at the heart of any good sci-fi
story, then, are people and their stories,

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.710
in which machines are only a part.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:46.651
Bradbury’s words go a long way in explaining
why we continually see stories in Cowboy Bebop

00:04:46.651 --> 00:04:50.500
that are surprisingly light on the technology
that surrounds them.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.750
Perhaps there’s no better example of this
than Faye Valentine.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:57.150
In many ways, Faye is a victim of science’s
progress.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.240
When she and her family travel to space aboard
their private shuttle, something goes horribly

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.220
wrong, leaving Faye as the only survivor.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:08.280
But her injuries are so severe that doctors
decide to cryogenically freeze her until she

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.000
can be saved by later medical advancements.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.390
Over fifty years pass, and Faye is awoken
by the questionably named physician, Dr. Baccus.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.600
Except there’s only one problem: “Then
tell me!

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.710
What kind of accident?

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.650
What was I like before then?

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.020
Who am I?!”

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.670
Yep, she lost her memory.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:28.880
Worse, because the Gate exploded while she
was frozen, there’s no surviving data on

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.830
who Faye even was before.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.420
"What is this?"

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:33.490
"An ID.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:44.462
This way if you die or if you forget yourself
they will know who you are and where you're

00:05:44.462 --> 00:05:45.462
from."

00:05:45.462 --> 00:05:46.462
"And I don't have that."

00:05:46.462 --> 00:05:47.462
And did we mention she’s now drowning in
debt?

00:05:47.462 --> 00:05:54.060
"I can't pay 300 million... it's unreasonable
for them to choose to resuscitate me and then

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.250
expect me to pay the debt."

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:59.381
Yet despite science and technology nearly
screwing Faye at every turn, Faye’s story

00:05:59.381 --> 00:06:02.910
is never folded into a warning about the future
or its technology.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:08.420
Instead, Faye’s journey is all about finding
a place to belong – a pretty common literary

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:09.940
trope across all genres.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:11.060
“My memory came back.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.120
But nothing good came out of it.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.950
There was no place for me to return to.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:22.400
This was the only place I could return to.”

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:28.040
Sure, elements of science fiction set up Faye’s
narrative, but at the end of the day, they’re

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.990
just silent cues that help move the conflict
along.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:35.230
By letting science elements do their work
in the background, Cowboy Bebop really thrusts

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.110
its characters into the spotlight.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:41.740
Which is maybe why, with only 10 out of 26
episodes exploring our heroes’ personal

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:45.190
stories, we’re still able to relate so much
to them.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:49.150
Seriously guys, who did not feel like crying
when Faye just lies down in the wreckage of

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.690
her childhood home.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:56.180
But sci-fi isn’t the only genre Cowboy Bebop
works in.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.270
"Wait."

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:04.699
Yeah, if the title wasn’t enough of a dead
giveaway, Cowboy Bebop also plays heavily

00:07:04.699 --> 00:07:06.760
with Western conventions.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:11.130
More than anything, the first session, Asteroid
Blues, really sets the tone.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:15.880
In the episode, Jet and Spike hunt down the
drug dealer Asimov Solensan and his girlfriend,

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:16.960
Katerina, in…

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:17.960
Tijuana.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:21.860
There are saloon fights and stetson hats,
indigenous wise men.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:25.310
We even see Spike wearing a poncho and sombrero
at one point.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:30.880
And true to form, even the episode’s ending
itself is an homage to classic Western themes.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:36.090
Katerina, realizing that Jet and the authorities
are closing in on them, decides to take drastic

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:39.460
action.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:42.840
“Adios.”

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:47.710
As literary critic Peter French notes, while
rare, when suicide is depicted in Westerns,

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:52.190
it “is shown to be the escape of the weak
or the defeated.”

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:56.630
For French, though, nothing characterizes
Westerns more than death, and that’s particularly

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.669
true in Cowboy Bebop.

00:07:58.669 --> 00:07:59.669
French writes,

00:07:59.669 --> 00:08:03.450
“The Western is about… a dead man’s
walk, or run or gallop.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:08.510
It is not accidental that so many of the heroes
and villains of Westerns are portrayed as

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:13.840
having a previous history in the American
Civil War on the losing Confederate side.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.639
They have been defeated and lived with death
and gore in a cause they, we are led to believe,

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:22.840
saw either as their inescapable duty or as
romantic.”

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:26.750
"Figure a man's only good for one oath at
a time.

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:30.639
I took mine to the confederate states of America.

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:32.190
So did you, Sam."

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:36.769
This concept of a dead man’s walk becomes
particularly relevant with Spike.

00:08:36.769 --> 00:08:41.459
Just as Western heroes aimlessly wander through
life carrying the burden of their romantic

00:08:41.459 --> 00:08:46.070
cause, which has now been extinguished, so,
too, does Spike meander through life carrying

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.079
his tragic romance with him.

00:08:48.079 --> 00:08:50.550
A romance that almost killed him.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.959
The first detail we learn about Spike is that
he’s already died once before.

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:56.699
“Again, I see?”

00:08:56.699 --> 00:08:57.699
“Again?”

00:08:57.699 --> 00:08:59.600
“I’ve already died once.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.790
Got killed by a woman.”

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:05.810
A former hitman for the Red Dragon Syndicate,
Spike wanted to escape with the love of his

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:09.550
life, Julia, after performing one last job.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:11.420
Except Julia never showed up.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:15.920
And Spike’s partner, well, let’s just
say he’s named Vicious for a reason.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.619
In the end, Spike barely managed to escape
with his life, with everyone in his old life

00:09:20.619 --> 00:09:22.079
believing he died.

00:09:22.079 --> 00:09:25.829
But instead of washing his hands of everything,
Spike holds on.

00:09:25.829 --> 00:09:27.459
"I'm gonna go look."

00:09:27.459 --> 00:09:28.459
"What?"

00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:30.329
"I'm gonna look for my woman."

00:09:30.329 --> 00:09:35.240
Just as French described with Westerns, Spike
can never fully tear himself away from his

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:36.610
bloody past.

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.730
Whether it’s to bring a bounty on his former
boss,

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:39.730
"Then what?

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:40.730
You're Mao's?"

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.730
"I owe him one from before."

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:47.949
Or to chase after the woman he loves, or to
settle the score with Vicious, Spike returns

00:09:47.949 --> 00:09:50.790
time and time again to his past.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:55.270
Considering Spike nearly dies every time he
runs after his past, we’ve got to wonder

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:57.290
what duty could be so important.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:01.430
Especially since he once told Faye that the
past doesn’t matter anymore:

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:06.269
“I guess I don’t know anything about my
past anymore.”

00:10:06.269 --> 00:10:08.170
“Isn’t that something that really doesn’t
matter?”

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:10.170
“You think that way because you have a past.”

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:12.910
“No matter the past, you still have a future.”

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:17.180
Well, digging deeper into the genre, the answer
isn’t too much of a stretch.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:21.499
As literary critic Jane Tompkins notes in
her book West of Everything, the heroes and

00:10:21.499 --> 00:10:25.769
villains of Westerns aren’t concerned with
traditional Judeo-Christian ethics – the

00:10:25.769 --> 00:10:30.510
harsh reality of the desert and their outlaw
lifestyles won’t allow it.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.550
Instead, their moral code revolves around
honor.

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:37.869
As French elaborates, “What, after all,
is the value of residence in the Kingdom of

00:10:37.869 --> 00:10:40.800
Heaven, if the cost is one’s self-respect?”

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.829
We see this in almost every character from
Spike’s past, but most vividly in Vicious.

00:10:45.829 --> 00:10:50.079
Vicious, ever the sociopath, holds a might
is right attitude.

00:10:50.079 --> 00:10:54.209
Those who can’t or won’t fight, according
to Vicious, shouldn’t be allowed to live.

00:10:54.209 --> 00:10:57.329
It’s the reason why he killed his boss and
later staged a coup.

00:10:57.329 --> 00:11:01.990
It’s also the reason why he seeks to kill
Spike, the man who has renounced his violent

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:02.990
past.

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:08.410
“The same blood runs in you and me.

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:15.329
The blood of a beast who wanders, desiring
the blood of others.”

00:11:15.329 --> 00:11:16.329
“I’ve bled all of that blood away.”

00:11:16.329 --> 00:11:17.329
“Then why are you still alive?”

00:11:17.329 --> 00:11:20.509
But even Vicious seemingly wants a fair fight
with Spike.

00:11:20.509 --> 00:11:23.569
He passed up easy opportunities to kill his
mortal foe.

00:11:23.569 --> 00:11:28.490
When Spike was passed out on one of Jupiter’s
moons, Vicious leaves him unharmed: "Tranquilizer,

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:29.490
huh?

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.449
They're mocking me."

00:11:31.449 --> 00:11:35.879
And when Spike and Vicious are in their final
duel, they both pass their own preferred weapons

00:11:35.879 --> 00:11:39.550
to each other, instead of either taking the
advantage.

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:43.839
As for Spike, his decision to kill Vicious
isn’t about him being a serious piece of

00:11:43.839 --> 00:11:44.839
sh*t.

00:11:44.839 --> 00:11:49.439
In part, it’s about righting a personal
wrong: Julia’s death: As French reminds

00:11:49.439 --> 00:11:54.319
us, “[the hero of a Western] is not the
cowboy equivalent of the Caped Crusader.

00:11:54.319 --> 00:11:59.129
The wrongs must always relate to some harm
suffered either by him directly or by someone

00:11:59.129 --> 00:12:01.809
with whom he has established a relationship.”

00:12:01.809 --> 00:12:05.490
But there’s also a more philosophical reason
behind Spike’s decision.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.470
And it has to do with skepticism.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:12.249
Since leaving the Syndicate, Spike has wondered
if he ever really survived at all – wondering

00:12:12.249 --> 00:12:13.369
if what he saw was real.

00:12:13.369 --> 00:12:14.369
“Look at these eyes.

00:12:14.369 --> 00:12:20.879
One of them is a fake because I lost it in
an accident.

00:12:20.879 --> 00:12:31.079
Since then, I’ve been seeing the past in
one eye and the present in the other.

00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:33.769
I had believed that what I saw was not all
of reality.”

00:12:33.769 --> 00:12:37.649
Specifically, Spike continually floats the
idea that everything he sees could just be

00:12:37.649 --> 00:12:38.649
a dream.

00:12:38.649 --> 00:12:41.569
“I’m only watching a dream that I never
awakened from.”

00:12:41.569 --> 00:12:45.809
In this sense, Spike echoes the skepticism
of ancient Chinese philosopher, Zhuangzhi,

00:12:45.809 --> 00:12:51.809
who wrote: “Once upon a time, I, Zhuangzi,
dreamt I was a butterfly, fluttering hither

00:12:51.809 --> 00:12:55.209
and thither, to all intents and purposes a
butterfly.

00:12:55.209 --> 00:13:00.410
I was conscious only of my happiness as a
butterfly, unaware that I was Zhuangzi.

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:04.249
Soon I awaked, and there I was, veritably
myself again.

00:13:04.249 --> 00:13:08.769
Now I do not know whether I was then a man
dreaming I was a butterfly, or whether I am

00:13:08.769 --> 00:13:11.829
now a butterfly, dreaming I am a man.”

00:13:11.829 --> 00:13:15.939
Spike, struggling to make sense of life before
and after the Syndicate, wonders if maybe

00:13:15.939 --> 00:13:18.339
he never really left the Syndicate at all.

00:13:18.339 --> 00:13:20.339
He wonders if he really died that night.

00:13:20.339 --> 00:13:25.639
It’s only after losing the love of his life
that he accepts the cruel reality of the situation:

00:13:25.639 --> 00:13:27.610
he’s been awake the whole time.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:33.189
“I thought I was watching a dream that I
would never awake from.

00:13:33.189 --> 00:13:37.050
Before I knew it, the dream was all over.”

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:42.369
For Spike, his deadman’s walk isn’t just
to take revenge on Vicious, it’s paradoxically

00:13:42.369 --> 00:13:44.639
to show himself that he’s awake.

00:13:44.639 --> 00:13:45.929
Or as he tells Faye:

00:13:45.929 --> 00:13:48.179
“I’m not going there to die.

00:13:48.179 --> 00:13:51.049
I’m going there to see if I really am alive.”

00:13:51.049 --> 00:13:55.019
And while it’s up for debate whether Spike
dies after his final fight with Vicious, I’ve

00:13:55.019 --> 00:13:59.540
got to say, Spike would make Clint Eastwood
proud by storming Vicious’s hideout.

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:04.000
Oh, and wondering why Vicious and Spike can
go at it with a katana and a pistol, and the

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:05.790
whole fight still feels believable?

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:10.079
Yeah, it’s because “Ronin” films – you
know, Japanese period pieces that followed

00:14:10.079 --> 00:14:14.089
the lone samurai dispensing personal justice
– are pretty much the cultural equivalent

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:15.160
of Westerns.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:19.509
In fact, from the epic one-man armies to the
tense silence before the duel, Westerns and

00:14:19.509 --> 00:14:23.360
Ronins have created a fairly shared visual
and thematic identity.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:24.360
But that’s another video.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:30.179
Finally, the third and perhaps most notable
genre Cowboy Bebop works in is noir.

00:14:30.179 --> 00:14:34.420
Strangely, noir is notoriously hard for film
critics to define.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:39.720
For example, Raymond Borde and Etienne Chaumeton
defined it in their seminal text, A Panorama

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:46.490
of American Film Noir, as having five key
characteristics: dreamlike, strange, erotic,

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:48.199
ambivalent, and cruel.

00:14:48.199 --> 00:14:52.119
But many popular noir films don’t hit all
the marks.

00:14:52.119 --> 00:14:54.850
While every character is influenced by film
noir.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:59.819
“Here’s lookin' at… my reflection, kid.”

00:14:59.819 --> 00:15:03.529
I think the genre has the greatest pull on
Faye and Jet.

00:15:03.529 --> 00:15:04.629
(Sorry, Spike.)

00:15:04.629 --> 00:15:08.800
Faye is clearly a femme fatale – one film
noir’s favorite conventions.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:14.439
See, when film noir first captured the popular
imagination following WWII, there were few

00:15:14.439 --> 00:15:19.209
representations of women that depicted them
as anything other than swooning, fainting,

00:15:19.209 --> 00:15:20.970
walking pairs of ovaries.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:25.869
But as literary scholar Janey Place notes,
noir changed that and became “one of the

00:15:25.869 --> 00:15:32.240
few periods of film in which women are active,
not static symbols, are intelligent and powerful,

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:37.459
if destructively so, and derive power, not
weakness, from their sexuality.”

00:15:37.459 --> 00:15:41.269
Fay is definitely intelligent, powerful, and
sexual.

00:15:41.269 --> 00:15:43.470
She’s constantly one step ahead of her male
peers:

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:47.569
“She sucked out all the antifreeze from
the radiator.

00:15:47.569 --> 00:15:54.170
It’ll be out for at least half a day.”

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:56.107
“Even if she didn’t pull this stunt, we
wouldn’t look for her, right?”

00:15:56.107 --> 00:15:57.107
“She emptied out the safe as well.”

00:15:57.107 --> 00:15:59.579
And she isn’t above using her sexuality
to get what she wants.

00:15:59.579 --> 00:16:00.759
“Hi.”

00:16:00.759 --> 00:16:03.520
“What do you want?

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:04.520
Ya wanna get some?”

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:05.520
“I’d rather be the one to give some.”

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.220
“I’m fine with that.”

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:12.089
“Hands in the air, Decker.”

00:16:12.089 --> 00:16:16.860
And while some, like critic Julie Grossman,
have criticized the trope for being an overly

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:22.389
simplistic reduction of lady agency in the
world, Faye is a little more dynamic.

00:16:22.389 --> 00:16:26.850
In fact, she’s the only character who really
makes a substantive internal change by the

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:27.980
series’ end.

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:33.939
But her narrative arc also taps into the very
philosophical spine of noir: nihilism.

00:16:33.939 --> 00:16:39.689
Yes – I know nihilism has become Wisecrack’s
favorite dead horse, but hey, I didn’t put

00:16:39.689 --> 00:16:40.689
it in the zeitgeist.

00:16:40.689 --> 00:16:45.319
According to literary critic Mark Conrad,
noir developed as a consequence of the 20th

00:16:45.319 --> 00:16:48.040
century ushering out old belief systems.

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:51.859
When Nietzsche famously proclaimed, “God
is dead”, he was making a statement that

00:16:51.859 --> 00:16:57.819
these seemingly eternal systems – like God,
morality, or even truth – were illusions.

00:16:57.819 --> 00:17:03.839
Reality, to Nietzsche, is in constant flux,
which means things like definitions, constants,

00:17:03.839 --> 00:17:06.500
and even being don’t exist over time.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:10.370
Whether he was right or wrong, Nietzsche was
correct in predicting the mass alienation

00:17:10.370 --> 00:17:14.120
and loneliness brought on by the disillusionment
of these systems.

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:18.280
As science chipped away at our happy belief
systems in the 20th century, people became

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.890
lost and anxious, and Wisecrack was born.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:26.539
Conrad writes “Seeing noir as a response
or reaction to the death of God helps explain

00:17:26.539 --> 00:17:31.720
the commonality of the elements that critics
have noted in noir films… the inherent pessimism,

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.380
alienation, and disorientation.”

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:39.279
And if there’s one character who’s continually
had belief system after belief system ripped

00:17:39.279 --> 00:17:41.200
away from her, it’s Faye.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:44.770
When Faye first awakes, she doesn’t actually
believe that she’s in the future.

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:48.019
That is until her lawyers tells her that she’s
got it all wrong.

00:17:48.019 --> 00:17:52.269
"No, it is definitely 2068 now.

00:17:52.269 --> 00:17:54.090
And that's not a TV.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:55.360
But a washing machine."

00:17:55.360 --> 00:18:00.490
And if waking up in a unrecognizable future
sounds bad, try adding trust issues.

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:04.779
When Faye finds out that her old lawyer you
know, the one she fell in love with had faked

00:18:04.779 --> 00:18:09.350
his death you can bet your bottom dollar that
she felt the same lonely, anxiety that Nietzsche

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:10.350
had described.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:13.309
“Where does the truth end, and the lies
start?”

00:18:13.309 --> 00:18:17.639
And Faye experiences the ultimate disillusionment
when she finds out that the goal she’s been

00:18:17.639 --> 00:18:20.390
searching for – her past – is gone.

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:25.090
After Ed takes Faye to the place where she
grew up, the two are greeted by an old classmate,

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:26.549
emphasis on old.

00:18:26.549 --> 00:18:28.600
“Are you…

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:29.620
Faye?

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:30.640
Impossible…

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:31.669
Really?

00:18:31.669 --> 00:18:35.760
It’s me, Sally Yung.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:39.970
I was in your high school graduating class.”

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:44.289
Time, like Nietzsche’s idea of reality,
is in constant motion, and it's left Faye

00:18:44.289 --> 00:18:45.500
behind.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:49.630
Realizing that she can never get her old life
back, Faye calls herself a ghost.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:52.350
"I bet you don't know.

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:54.530
Actually, she's --" "A ghost."

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:59.139
As she later tells Spike, the life that she
thought she wanted – the home that she wanted

00:18:59.139 --> 00:19:03.559
to return to – is no more: Faye’s not
the only noir-esque character.

00:19:03.559 --> 00:19:07.070
Who’s a trench coat-wearing cop who remembers
his sweetheart like this?

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:09.950
Yeah, it’s everyone’s favorite chef, Jet.

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:13.330
Like Faye, Jet’s backdrop borrows heavily
from noir conventions.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:17.510
A former cop, Jet suffered two massive blows
in his past.

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:20.320
First, his lover walked out on him without
warning.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:30.100
“When I came home, only that watch was left,
and a small note with one word: 'farewell'.”

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:33.480
And second, he was nearly killed when he cornered
a Syndicate assassin.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:35.450
Oh, and did I mention Jet’s nickname?

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:36.610
“To think you’re a bounty hunter.

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:43.160
You, who used to be called the Black Dog – a
mad dog who never let go of things he'd bitten

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:44.160
into.”

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:48.760
So, we’ve got a former cop turned PI, I
mean bounty hunter, who’s pining over a

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:49.799
lost love.

00:19:49.799 --> 00:19:51.360
Yeah, it’s noir.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:56.549
Like Faye, Jet has to learn that time moves
forward – and with it, everyone else.

00:19:56.549 --> 00:20:00.610
Consider the pocket watch that Jet’s lover,
Alisa, left him.

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:15.890
“You seem to think that time on Ganymede
had stopped…

00:20:15.890 --> 00:20:21.039
That’s a story from long ago...

00:20:21.039 --> 00:20:22.039
I’ve... forgotten it.

00:20:22.039 --> 00:20:23.039
I don’t need time that stands still.”

00:20:23.039 --> 00:20:27.539
While the symbolism might be a bit heavy-handed,
here, Jet holding on to the broken watch represents

00:20:27.539 --> 00:20:32.610
his desire to return to the past, to a time
when his watch still ticked, and Alisa waited

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.330
for him to come home from work.

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:39.029
But, when Jet seeks answers about the past,
he's reminded that it's long gone.

00:20:39.029 --> 00:20:43.730
Alisa has a new life and a new boyfriend,
and only Jet is wondering why.

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:48.630
Worse, when Alisa finally gives Jet the answers
he's looking for, they shatter his ideas of

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:53.820
the past; as it turns out, Jet's relationship
wasn't so perfect, and Alisa felt trapped.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.429
"You were like this back then, too...

00:20:56.429 --> 00:20:57.549
You decided everything.

00:20:57.549 --> 00:20:58.549
And you were always right.

00:20:58.549 --> 00:21:01.250
When I was with you, I never had to do anything.

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:12.080
All I had to do was hang on to your arm like
a child, with no cares in the world.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:17.320
In the end, Jet can’t return to his past,
because this idealized past never existed

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:18.809
at all.

00:21:18.809 --> 00:21:23.259
Looking back at that interstitial about playing
without fear of risky things, breaking traditional

00:21:23.259 --> 00:21:25.230
styles, and creating new genres.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:30.220
It’s hard to argue that series director
Shinichirō Watanabe didn’t do just that

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:31.779
in Cowboy Bebop.

00:21:31.779 --> 00:21:36.970
Sci-fi, westerns, noir – even a little ronin
action – it’s all here and it’s all

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:37.970
excellent.

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:41.740
It’s this very genre-bending that’s made
the series so popular, and it’s the reason

00:21:41.740 --> 00:22:45.930
why Cowboy Bebop seems so fresh two decades
later.

